{"subjectId":"java","id":"collectors","title":"Collectors & groupingBy","group":"Functional Java & Standard Library","order":53,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>collect(...)</code> is the terminal operation that turns a stream into a collection, map, string or summary — and <code>Collectors</code> is the toolbox of recipes for it.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Collector</strong> — a recipe of four functions — <em>supplier</em> (new container), <em>accumulator</em> (add an element), <em>combiner</em> (merge two containers, for parallel runs) and <em>finisher</em> — that <code>Stream.collect</code> uses to perform a mutable reduction.</p></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list\"></i>Into lists and sets</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Call</th><th>Result</th><th>Mutable?</th><th>Nulls?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>stream.toList()</code> (16+)</td><td><code>List&lt;T&gt;</code></td><td><strong>No</strong> — unmodifiable</td><td>allowed</td></tr><tr><td><code>collect(Collectors.toList())</code></td><td><code>List&lt;T&gt;</code> (an <code>ArrayList</code> in practice)</td><td>yes (not guaranteed)</td><td>allowed</td></tr><tr><td><code>collect(Collectors.toUnmodifiableList())</code> (10+)</td><td><code>List&lt;T&gt;</code></td><td>No</td><td><strong>NPE</strong></td></tr><tr><td><code>collect(Collectors.toSet())</code></td><td><code>Set&lt;T&gt;</code> (a <code>HashSet</code>)</td><td>yes</td><td>allowed</td></tr><tr><td><code>collect(Collectors.toCollection(TreeSet::new))</code></td><td>the exact type you choose</td><td>yes</td><td>depends</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\nimport java.util.stream.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        List&lt;String&gt; fixed = Stream.of(\"b\", \"a\").toList();\n        List&lt;String&gt; growable = Stream.of(\"b\", \"a\").collect(Collectors.toList());\n        growable.add(\"c\");\n        System.out.println(growable);\n        try {\n            fixed.add(\"c\");\n        } catch (UnsupportedOperationException e) {\n            System.out.println(\"toList() result is unmodifiable\");\n        }\n        TreeSet&lt;String&gt; sorted = Stream.of(\"pear\", \"apple\", \"fig\", \"apple\")\n                .collect(Collectors.toCollection(TreeSet::new));\n        System.out.println(sorted);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>[b, a, c]\ntoList() result is unmodifiable\n[apple, fig, pear]</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-map\"></i>Into maps: <code>toMap</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\nimport java.util.stream.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        List&lt;String&gt; words = List.of(\"apple\", \"avocado\", \"banana\", \"cherry\");\n\n        Map&lt;String, Integer&gt; lengths = words.stream()\n                .collect(Collectors.toMap(w -&gt; w, String::length, (a, b) -&gt; a, TreeMap::new));\n        System.out.println(lengths);\n\n        try {\n            words.stream().collect(Collectors.toMap(w -&gt; w.charAt(0), w -&gt; w));   // 'a' twice\n        } catch (IllegalStateException e) {\n            System.out.println(e.getMessage());\n        }\n\n        // merge function decides what to do with duplicate keys\n        Map&lt;Character, String&gt; byLetter = words.stream()\n                .collect(Collectors.toMap(w -&gt; w.charAt(0), w -&gt; w, (a, b) -&gt; a + \"|\" + b, TreeMap::new));\n        System.out.println(byLetter);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>{apple=5, avocado=7, banana=6, cherry=6}\nDuplicate key a (attempted merging values apple and avocado)\n{a=apple|avocado, b=banana, c=cherry}</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>toMap(keyFn, valueFn)</code> throws <code>IllegalStateException: Duplicate key</code> on the first collision, and <code>NullPointerException</code> if a <em>value</em> is <code>null</code>. Pass a merge function whenever keys might repeat.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-table\"></i><code>groupingBy</code> — the SQL <code>GROUP BY</code> of Java</h3>\n<p><code>groupingBy(classifier)</code> builds a <code>Map&lt;K, List&lt;T&gt;&gt;</code>. The two optional arguments are the <strong>map factory</strong> and a <strong>downstream collector</strong> applied to each group.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\nimport java.util.stream.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    record Emp(String name, String dept, int salary) {}\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        List&lt;Emp&gt; emps = List.of(\n                new Emp(\"Asha\", \"Eng\", 120), new Emp(\"Ravi\", \"HR\", 70),\n                new Emp(\"Zoe\", \"Eng\", 100), new Emp(\"Mohan\", \"Sales\", 80),\n                new Emp(\"Lena\", \"HR\", 75));\n\n        Map&lt;String, List&lt;String&gt;&gt; names = emps.stream().collect(Collectors.groupingBy(\n                Emp::dept, TreeMap::new, Collectors.mapping(Emp::name, Collectors.toList())));\n        System.out.println(names);\n\n        Map&lt;String, Long&gt; counts = emps.stream().collect(\n                Collectors.groupingBy(Emp::dept, TreeMap::new, Collectors.counting()));\n        System.out.println(counts);\n\n        Map&lt;String, Integer&gt; payroll = emps.stream().collect(\n                Collectors.groupingBy(Emp::dept, TreeMap::new, Collectors.summingInt(Emp::salary)));\n        System.out.println(payroll);\n\n        Map&lt;String, Double&gt; avg = emps.stream().collect(\n                Collectors.groupingBy(Emp::dept, TreeMap::new, Collectors.averagingInt(Emp::salary)));\n        System.out.println(avg);\n\n        Map&lt;String, Optional&lt;Emp&gt;&gt; top = emps.stream().collect(Collectors.groupingBy(\n                Emp::dept, TreeMap::new, Collectors.maxBy(Comparator.comparingInt(Emp::salary))));\n        top.forEach((d, e) -&gt; System.out.println(d + \" -&gt; \" + e.get().name()));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>{Eng=[Asha, Zoe], HR=[Ravi, Lena], Sales=[Mohan]}\n{Eng=2, HR=2, Sales=1}\n{Eng=220, HR=145, Sales=80}\n{Eng=110.0, HR=72.5, Sales=80.0}\nEng -&gt; Asha\nHR -&gt; Lena\nSales -&gt; Mohan</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Deterministic order</div><p>Plain <code>groupingBy(f)</code> returns a <code>HashMap</code>, so the key order you print is not something to rely on. Pass <code>TreeMap::new</code> (sorted) or <code>LinkedHashMap::new</code> (encounter order) when order matters.</p></div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Downstream collector</th><th>Per group you get</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>counting()</code></td><td><code>Long</code> count</td></tr><tr><td><code>summingInt(f)</code> / <code>averagingDouble(f)</code></td><td>sum / average</td></tr><tr><td><code>mapping(f, toList())</code></td><td>a list of a projected field</td></tr><tr><td><code>maxBy(cmp)</code> / <code>minBy(cmp)</code></td><td><code>Optional&lt;T&gt;</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>toSet()</code>, <code>joining(\", \")</code></td><td>set / concatenated string</td></tr><tr><td><code>collectingAndThen(c, fn)</code></td><td>post-process, e.g. unwrap the <code>Optional</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>groupingBy(...)</code></td><td>nested (multi-level) grouping</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-split-2\"></i><code>partitioningBy</code>, <code>joining</code> and friends</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\nimport java.util.stream.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        List&lt;Integer&gt; nums = List.of(1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7);\n\n        Map&lt;Boolean, List&lt;Integer&gt;&gt; evenOdd = nums.stream()\n                .collect(Collectors.partitioningBy(n -&gt; n % 2 == 0));\n        System.out.println(evenOdd);\n\n        System.out.println(Stream.of(\"a\", \"b\", \"c\").collect(Collectors.joining(\", \", \"[\", \"]\")));\n\n        IntSummaryStatistics st = nums.stream().collect(Collectors.summarizingInt(n -&gt; n));\n        System.out.println(st);\n\n        Map&lt;Integer, Long&gt; byLength = Stream.of(\"hi\", \"yo\", \"hey\", \"java\", \"sun\")\n                .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(String::length, TreeMap::new, Collectors.counting()));\n        System.out.println(byLength);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>{false=[1, 3, 5, 7], true=[2, 4, 6]}\n[a, b, c]\nIntSummaryStatistics{count=7, sum=28, min=1, average=4.000000, max=7}\n{2=2, 3=2, 4=1}</code></pre></figure>\n<p><code>partitioningBy</code> always has exactly two keys, <code>false</code> and <code>true</code> — even if one group is empty — which makes it nicer than <code>groupingBy</code> with a boolean.</p>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Stream.toList() vs Collectors.toList()?</summary><div><p><code>Stream.toList()</code> (Java 16) returns an <strong>unmodifiable</strong> list and accepts <code>null</code> elements. <code>Collectors.toList()</code> returns a list with no guarantees on type or mutability (an <code>ArrayList</code> in practice, so callers often mutate it). Prefer <code>toList()</code> for results you only read; use <code>toCollection(ArrayList::new)</code> when you need to mutate.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How would you count word frequencies with streams?</summary><div><p><code>words.stream().collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Function.identity(), Collectors.counting()))</code> → <code>Map&lt;String, Long&gt;</code>. Add <code>TreeMap::new</code> as the middle argument for sorted keys.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What happens with duplicate keys in Collectors.toMap?</summary><div><p>Without a merge function it throws <code>IllegalStateException</code> (\"Duplicate key …\"). Supply the third argument, e.g. <code>(a, b) -&gt; a</code> to keep the first or <code>Integer::sum</code> to add counts; a fourth argument chooses the map implementation.</p></div></details></section>"}